 "critic": "Three families of cards read as generated. (1) The short Friday-fee card ('X was at the stall again for the Friday fee the Claws collect, and I am short. Can you do something about it?') repeats word for word with only the names swapped, and its ask is vague. (2) The terse bounty cards (stolen jacket or credstick, follow someone, find someone missing, collect a debt, escort, sealed case, remove a gang member) all follow a fixed 'situation, task, price' format with no stated cause and no local detail. (3) The longer cards share stock parts: a 'three options' sentence ('X, Y, or Z'), a closing line about reputation or trust ('People trust you with their things around here', 'You will not know me from anyone'), and stock cause clauses ('the thief is no one from this row', 'I had never seen them before'). Several are exact duplicates, and the closing lines sometimes sit oddly against the situation. The cards that read as written by a person carry specific, local pressure: Ivo Popov's collector and stalls starting to say no, the patrols watching curfew and not pickpockets, and the crews watching each other and nobody else.",
 "sheet": 2
}